1321:| OD31 | <a id="od31"></a>ARITH-2: no `Int` fallback for an unconstrained literal (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | an unconstrained integer literal is `Int` and a float literal `Float64`; bug 327 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): an integer literal takes its type from its context, as today; the fallback to `Int` where nothing is expected is removed, so an unconstrained literal is refused; suffixes are not required; float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Now ARITH-2@2 (partial, bug 327) |
1338:| OD48 | <a id="od48"></a>The index type of a counted loop over two literals (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type (write `for i from 0 until n` with a typed bound, or annotate); (b) the index is `Int` there. Recommendation: (a), consistent with OD31 Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type; a typed bound supplies the other bound's type, in either order | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair (STMT-6) | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused, as OD31 |
1353:| OD63 | <a id="od63"></a>Float literals take their expected type (added 2026-10-30, item Q4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a float literal takes the float type its position expects and is refused where nothing is expected, as ARITH-2@2 states under OD31's note that float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Options: (a) yes, as ARITH-2@2 states for integers and floats alike; (b) a float literal defaults to `f64`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not checked | `let x = 1.5;` is accepted as `Float64` (ARITH-2@2 is partial, bug 327) | ARITH-2, ARITH-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Expected type, refused where none |
1354:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
1373:| OD83 | <a id="od83"></a>The call-site allocator binding in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the call-site allocator binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | TRUST-4, MEM-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No call-site binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Under the authority decision, one allocator form per target: hosted, `with(Alloc)` and no allocator value; freestanding, an `Arena` parameter plus `with(Alloc)`. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: one form on every target, an `Allocator<A>` value plus `with(Alloc)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-allocation)) |
1427:| OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII |
1428:| OD138 | <a id="od138"></a>Which positions give a literal its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which positions give an integer literal operand its expected type under ARITH-2@3 (and a float literal's, should OPEN-63 give floats the same rule). Bears on ARITH-2, TYPE-4 and TYPE-6. The current compiler: an unconstrained integer literal is typed `Int` and a float literal `Float64` (bug 327); with `x: u8`, a literal beside `x` takes `u8` as the other operand of `+`, `<` and `&` and as the count of `>>`: `x + 300`, `x < 300`, `x & 300` and `x >> 300` are each refused natively with E0227 (`integer literal 300 is out of range for type 'u8'`), and `300 as Int` is accepted, whether through `as` or the `Int` default the probe does not show. ARITH-2@3 refuses an integer literal with no expected type but does not list the positions that supply one. Options: (a) declared positions (an annotated binding, a parameter, a return, a field) and the other operand of a binary operator, as the probe shows the current compiler doing for `+`, `<`, `&` and a shift count (other operators were not probed); (b) declared positions only, so a literal beside a typed operand needs a typed binding; (c) option (a), plus the target type of an `as` for its literal operand. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-138 | see OPEN-138 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Declared positions + other operand of a binary op |
1702:- <a id="OPEN-137"></a>**OPEN-137** (OD137, Character literals outside ASCII): See [OD137](#od137).
1768:- <a id="OPEN-138"></a>**OPEN-138** (OD138, Which positions give a literal its expected type): See [OD138](#od138).
