legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:18:## Rules
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:21:### SYN-1. Decided syntax governs the derived grammar
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:117:The token forms (SYN-6), the statement `match` (SYN-8), the value `if` (SYN-9) and the separators
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:279:### SYN-2. Operators group by a fixed precedence
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:325:### SYN-3. Compiler spellings are reserved
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:370:### SYN-4. A struct literal in a condition head is parenthesized
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:425:<a id="SYN-5"></a>
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:426:### SYN-5. Source text and string literals are UTF-8
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:428:**SYN-5@3.** A source file is UTF-8 text, a string literal denotes the UTF-8 encoding of its characters, and its only escapes are `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"` and `\0`, so a file that is not valid UTF-8 and any other `\` sequence in a literal are not accepted, and every `String<A>` reachable by safe code holds valid UTF-8.
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:453:<a id="SYN-6"></a>
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:454:### SYN-6. Tokens
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:456:**SYN-6@3.** A token is an identifier (a letter or `_`, then letters, digits and `_`), an integer literal in decimal or with a `0x`, `0b` or `0o` prefix, a float literal written as digits, `.` and digits, a string literal, a char literal (one ASCII character, or one of the escapes `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\0`, `\\` and `\'`, between single quotes), a label (`'` then an identifier), a keyword, or an operator or punctuation mark, and `//` begins a comment that ends at the end of its line.
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:494:### SYN-7. `>>` closes two type-argument lists
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:533:### SYN-8. A `match` that begins a statement is a statement
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:571:### SYN-9. A value `if` has an `else`
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:603:### SYN-10. The separators `.` and `::`
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:657:## Version history
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:663:- SYN-6@2 (spec audits 6 and 10, 2026-10-30) adds the char-literal escapes to the sentence; the compiler accepted them before.
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:667:- SYN-6@3 (2026-11-03) brings the chapter into line with the recorded owner decisions; SYN-6@2 is superseded by version.
legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md:669:- SYN-5@2 (2026-11-03) supersedes SYN-5@1 for decided-language conformance.
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:13:## 1. Reading the grammar and slices
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:53:## 2. Lexical grammar
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:55:### Encoding, space, comments, names
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:57:UTF-8 source and UTF-8 static text are supported by [Core Q4.1–Q4.4](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core); the explicit legacy source-encoding and escape rule is [SYN-5](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-5). ASCII char literals are decided by [OD137](../docs/decisions.md#od137). Exact source whitespace, identifier alphabet and source byte edge cases lack a dedicated decision: **OPEN-1**, **OPEN-2**. Recommended lexical core:
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:69:Slices: identifier/space/source decoding 0; line comments recommended at 0; labels 4. `//` comments have no nesting; `/*…*/` is absent in [SYN-6](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-6), but its precise status as a current owner spelling choice is **OPEN-1**. LF, CR or EOF ends a comment; CRLF therefore ends it at CR, with LF remaining whitespace (OPEN-1 recommendation). A BOM, shebang, non-ASCII whitespace, Unicode identifiers, embedded source NUL are not silently accepted by this recommendation; see OPEN-1/2.
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:73:### Reserved list
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:90:### Numbers and quoted literals
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:105:Decimal integers arrive at 0; other bases at 2; FLOAT at 26; STRING/CHAR at 16 (a reason STRING in an earlier annotation is metadata, not a runtime StaticStr feature). Leading-zero decimal integers are explicitly refused: [slice-0 surface](../docs/decisions.md#decided-slice0-surface-2026-11-06). The old forms are documented by [SYN-6](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-6). **OPEN-5**: approve lowercase base prefixes, no separators, float digit/point syntax and a minimal typed-integer suffix policy and boundary handling; the frozen lexer also accepted uppercase prefixes. The [approved Q6.2 field example](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields) contains `200u8`. A blanket suffix refusal conflicts with that example; OPEN-5 recommends retaining typed integer suffixes, but their complete width/base/boundary grammar needs approval. The displayed INT production is the unsuffixed kernel, not a complete suffix grammar. No exponent notation, `1.`, `.5`, hex floats or special NaN/Inf literals are proposed.
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:109:Only these escapes are proposed, copied exactly from SYN-5/6; **OPEN-6** requests explicit owner ratification where decisions.md does not enumerate them:
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:113:| string | `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"`, `\0` | UTF-8 characters other than quote/backslash; raw line-break/control policy OPEN-6 |
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:114:| char | `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\0`, `\\`, `\'` | exactly one ASCII character other than quote/backslash; controls OPEN-6 |
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:116:No `\xHH`, `\u{…}`, interpolation, raw-string delimiter or byte-string prefix is admitted by this proposal. Embedded NUL in static text is explicitly permitted by Core Q4.2, using `\0`; ASCII literal restrictions do not restrict every computed `char` to 0…127 ([TYPE-20](../legacy/docs/spec/types.md#TYPE-20)). A char ending in a closing quote takes priority over LABEL; a bare apostrophe plus a name is LABEL. The exact lexical distinction and malformed-quote refusal is OPEN-6, not a token-level LL(1) claim about individual bytes.
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:118:### Punctuation and operators
docs/proposals/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:152:## 3. Items, attributes, generics and types
